            indications = "Analgesic adjunct in general anesthesia induction and maintenance; Regional anesthesia adjuvant (intrathecal / epidural for labor analgesia, post-operative analgesia); Monitored Anesthesia Care (MAC) conscious sedation (combined with midazolam or propofol); Breakthrough cancer pain (transmucosal/transdermal).",
            doses = "Intravenous General Anesthesia:\n• Pre-induction / Pre-medication: 1.0 to 2.0 mcg/kg IV (50 to 100 mcg) 3-5 min before induction to blunt laryngoscopy sympathetic response.\n• Maintenance: 1.0 to 3.0 mcg/kg/hr IV intermittent boluses (25 to 50 mcg) or continuous infusion 0.5 to 2.0 mcg/kg/hr.\n• Major Cardiac / High-Dose Anesthesia: 20 to 50 mcg/kg IV with 100% O2.\n\nNeuraxial Adjuvant:\n• Intrathecal (Spinal): 10 to 25 mcg (0.2 to 0.5 mL) combined with 0.5% Hyperbaric Bupivacaine (provides intense sensory block without motor prolongation).\n• Epidural: 50 to 100 mcg in 10 mL saline or local anesthetic solution for labor or post-op analgesia.\n\nPediatric Induction/Analgesia:\n• 1.0 to 2.0 mcg/kg IV slow push.",
            administration = "Administer IV slowly over 1 to 2 minutes. Rapid bolus injection (<30 seconds) triggers severe glottic closure and chest wall rigidity. Naloxone (0.4 mg IV) must be immediately ready at bedside.",
            timing = "Hospital Operating Room / ICU intravenous or neuraxial administration only (शल्यक्रिया कक्ष वा ICU मा मात्र प्रयोग गर्ने).",
            specialInstructions = "Lipid solubility is ~600 times greater than morphine; rapid onset (1-2 minutes) and short duration of single small doses (30-60 min) due to rapid redistribution from brain to adipose tissues. Context-sensitive half-time increases significantly with prolonged infusions (>2-3 hours).",
            pkPd = "Highly lipophilic phenylpiperidine. Rapid blood-brain barrier penetration. Protein binding ~80-85% (mainly alpha-1 acid glycoprotein). Rapidly redistributed from highly perfused organs to muscle and fat. Extensively metabolized by hepatic CYP3A4 via N-dealkylation to inactive norfentanyl; <10% excreted unchanged in urine. Elimination half-life ~2 to 4 hours.",
            renalAdj = "No major reduction needed for single doses; monitor for drug accumulation and prolonged respiratory depression with repeated doses or continuous infusions in severe renal failure.",
