            blackBoxWarning = null,
            indications = "Pre-operative antisialagogue to reduce salivary, tracheobronchial, and pharyngeal secretions prior to intubation; Protection against reflex intraoperative bradycardia (vagal reflexes from peritoneal traction, oculocardiac reflex, carotid sinus stimulation); Co-administration with Neostigmine to counteract muscarinic side effects (bradycardia, bronchospasm, excessive secretions) during neuromuscular blockade reversal; Treatment of drug-induced bradycardia.",
            doses = "Pre-operative Secretion Reduction (Antisialagogue):\n• Adult: 0.1 to 0.2 mg IV or IM (4 to 5 mcg/kg) 30-60 min pre-op or at induction.\n• Pediatric: 4 to 8 mcg/kg IV or IM.\n\nNeuromuscular Blockade Reversal (with Neostigmine):\n• Adult & Child: 0.01 mg (10 mcg) Glycopyrrolate per 0.05 mg (50 mcg) Neostigmine IV (typical adult dose: 0.5 mg Glycopyrrolate with 2.5 mg Neostigmine IV administered simultaneously over 1 minute).\n\nIntraoperative Reflex Bradycardia Treatment:\n• Adult: 0.1 to 0.2 mg IV bolus repeated q2-3 min as needed (max 0.8 mg).\n• Pediatric: 4 to 10 mcg/kg IV.",
            administration = "Administer intravenously or intramuscularly without dilution. Compatible in the same syringe with Neostigmine. Incompatible in the same syringe with alkaline drugs (Thiopental, Methohexital).",
            timing = "Operating Room / Pre-op holding area IV/IM administration (शल्यक्रिया अगाडि वा मांसपेशी उल्टाउँदा दिने).",
            specialInstructions = "DOES NOT CROSS BLOOD-BRAIN BARRIER: Quaternary ammonium structure carries permanent positive charge; cannot penetrate BBB or placenta. Unlike Atropine or Scopolamine, glycopyrrolate causes virtually zero central anticholinergic syndrome (confusion, delirium, agitation) and produces minimal pupillary dilation (mydriasis).",
            pkPd = "Onset of action: IV <1 minute, IM 15-30 minutes. Peak vagal-blocking effect occurs in 3-5 minutes IV. Duration of antisialagogue effect is exceptionally prolonged: 7 to 8 hours (much longer than atropine). Elimination via renal excretion of unchanged drug (~85%) and biliary secretion. Elimination half-life ~1.2 to 1.7 hours.",
