            genericName = "Isoflurane",
            system = "Anesthesiology & Perioperative",
            drugClass = "Volatile Inhalation General Anesthetic / Halogenated Methyl Ethyl Ether",
            blackBoxWarning = "Administer only via calibrated vaporizers specifically designed for isoflurane by trained anesthesia personnel. MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA: Potent trigger for fatal malignant hyperthermia crisis. Can cause profound concentration-dependent respiratory depression and arterial hypotension. Ensure dantrolene and resuscitation facilities are immediately available.",
            indications = "Induction and maintenance of General Anesthesia for inpatient and outpatient surgical procedures; Sedation for refractory status epilepticus in intensive care units; Bronchodilator for refractory status asthmaticus.",
            doses = "Inhalation Anesthesia:\n• Minimum Alveolar Concentration (MAC in 100% O2):\n  - Adult (40y): 1.15%\n  - Elderly (70y): 1.00%\n  - Infant (1-6 months): 1.60%\n  - Neonate: 1.60%\n  - Addition of 70% N2O reduces MAC by ~50% (MAC becomes 0.50%).\n• Induction: 1.5% to 3.0% inspired concentration with oxygen (often causes coughing/laryngospasm due to pungency; IV induction with propofol preferred).\n• Maintenance: 1.0% to 2.5% inspired concentration when administered with nitrous oxide (N2O) and oxygen, or 1.5% to 3.0% with oxygen alone.",
            administration = "Administered solely by inhalation via an agent-specific, temperature-compensated, calibrated variable-bypass vaporizer (purple color-coded Isoflurane vaporizer). Pungent, ethereal odor makes mask induction difficult due to breath-holding and coughing.",
            timing = "Hospital Operating Room inhalation only (शल्यक्रिया कक्षमा भेपोराइजरबाट मात्र सुंघाउने).",
            specialInstructions = "NEURO-PROTECTIVE PROPERTIES: Isoflurane maintains cerebral autoregulation and decreases cerebral metabolic oxygen consumption (CMRO2) up to isoelectric EEG (at ~2 MAC), making it historically popular for neurovascular and carotid endarterectomy surgeries. Mild coronary vasodilation has been theoretically linked to 'coronary steal' in dogs, but extensive human clinical trials confirm it is safe in coronary disease when hypotension is prevented.",
